    {
      "dom_id": "question-164282",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "The projection of the intersection between the elliptic paraboloid $4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8$ and the plane \n$z=4$  onto the $xy$-plane is an ellipse. What are the lengths of the semi-axes of the ellipse?",
      "worked_solution": "To find the intersection of the paraboloid and the plane $z=4$, we substitute $z=4$ into the equation of the paraboloid, and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=z+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=4+8 \\\\ 4(x-5)^{2}+6(y+4)^{2}=12 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{3}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{2} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{(x-5)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{2}}+\\frac{(y+4)^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the equation of an ellipse with semi-axes of lengths $\\sqrt{3}$ and $\\sqrt{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
